  { id:'opmcq-c2-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"While examining a 30-year-old man, the ophthalmologist noticed that the focal lines of the 2 principal meridians are focused in front of the retina. What is the type of refractive error seen in this patient?",
    options:["Myopia","Hyperopia","Simple astigmatism","Compound astigmatism","Mixed astigmatism"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Two focal lines both lying in front of the retina is compound myopic astigmatism — both principal meridians are myopic, so both are named by the one word *compound*.** The stem gives the diagnosis away by arithmetic rather than by clue: the moment there are *two* focal lines instead of one focal point, the error is astigmatic, and where those two lines sit relative to the retina names the subtype.\n\nThe lecture defines the five types against the fovea and the wording maps straight onto this stem: \"**COMPOUND MYOPIC ASTIGMATISM:** Both focal points are located **in front of** the fovea\" (L7,8 p67). Its companion definitions settle every distractor:\n\n| Type | Where the two focal lines sit |\n|---|---|\n| Simple myopic | one on the fovea, the other in front |\n| Simple hyperopic | one on the fovea, the other behind |\n| Compound myopic | **both in front** |\n| Compound hyperopic | both behind |\n| Mixed | one in front, one behind |\n\n**A** and **B** are wrong for a reason that precedes the subtype question: myopia and hyperopia are *spherical* errors, and a spherical eye brings parallel rays to a single point, not to two focal lines. The deck's own definition of astigmatism is exactly this — rays \"will not uniformly focus to a single point on the retina but instead into two focal lines (a = no, stigma = point)\" (L7,8 p61). The stem's \"2 principal meridians\" with separate focal lines therefore rules both out before the position of the lines is even considered.\n\n**C, simple astigmatism**, requires one meridian to be emmetropic — \"One meridian is emmetropic, other meridian either myopic or hyperopic\" (L7,8 p64). An emmetropic meridian focuses *on* the retina, so simple astigmatism always has one line on the retina and one off it. Here neither line is on the retina, so it is not simple.\n\n**E, mixed astigmatism**, is the one that straddles: \"One meridian is myopic, other meridian hyperopic\" (L7,8 p64), which puts one focal line in front of the retina and one behind. That is Q2 of this chapter, not this one. The chapter deliberately runs the two side by side with identical option lists so that only the position of the lines separates them.\n\nThe patient's age carries no weight here — 30 is given for realism, not as a clue, and the deck attaches no age to any astigmatism subtype.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Compound astigmatism places both focal lines on the same side of the retina — both in front is compound myopic; simple leaves one line on the retina and mixed straddles it.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.16 (Ch.2 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"While examining a 22-year-old man, the ophthalmologist noticed that the focal lines of the 2 principal meridians are located on both sides of the retina. What is the type of refractive error seen in this patient?",
    options:["Myopia","Hyperopia","Simple astigmatism","Compound astigmatism","Mixed astigmatism"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**Focal lines on *both sides* of the retina — one in front, one behind — is mixed astigmatism.** The lecture's definition is verbatim this: \"**MIXED ASTIGMATISM:** One point of focus is **behind** the fovea, while the other is **in front of** the fovea\" (L7,8 p67), and its causes tree says the same thing in meridian language: mixed = \"One meridian is myopic, other meridian hyperopic\" (L7,8 p64). A myopic meridian focuses in front of the retina, a hyperopic one behind it, so the two descriptions are the same fact stated twice.\n\n**A and B** fail at the first hurdle, as in Q1: a purely spherical myopic or hyperopic eye produces one focal *point*, not two focal *lines*. Two lines mean the refracting surface has different power in different meridians, which is the definition of astigmatism (L7,8 p61).\n\n**C, simple astigmatism**, needs one emmetropic meridian, i.e. one focal line sitting *on* the retina (L7,8 p64). \"On both sides of the retina\" explicitly places neither line on it.\n\n**D, compound astigmatism**, is the near-miss and the reason this question is printed immediately after Q1: compound means both meridians err in the *same* direction, so both focal lines land on the *same* side of the retina — both in front (compound myopic) or both behind (compound hyperopic) (L7,8 p67). \"Both sides\" is the exact opposite of \"the same side\".\n\nA point of vocabulary the deck flags itself: it prints **three** regular types in its causes tree (simple, compound, mixed, L7,8 p64) but **five** in its figures (simple myopic, compound myopic, mixed, simple hyperopic, compound hyperopic, L7,8 p66). Both printings are the professor's; the five-way list simply expands simple and compound into their myopic and hyperopic variants, and mixed is the one type that needs no such expansion — it is inherently one of each.\n\nClinically, mixed astigmatism is the least forgiving of the regular types, because no amount of accommodation can bring both meridians onto the retina at once: accommodation moves *both* focal lines forward together, so relieving the hyperopic meridian drives the myopic one further in front. Correction is therefore spherocylindrical from the outset — a sphere to shift the pair, a cylinder to collapse the interval between them (see Q22 of this chapter). *(The accommodation argument is standard optics and is not taken from the course material; the deck states only that accommodation can compensate hypermetropia in younger patients, L7,8 p54.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Mixed astigmatism straddles the retina — one focal line in front and one behind — because one principal meridian is myopic and the other hyperopic.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.16 (Ch.2 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Which of the following is the type of lenses used to correct simple astigmatism?",
    options:["Cylindrical lenses only","Spherical lenses only","A & B together","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Simple astigmatism needs a cylinder alone, because only one meridian is out of focus.** The lecture defines it as \"One meridian is emmetropic, other meridian either myopic or hyperopic\" (L7,8 p64) — the emmetropic meridian already focuses on the retina and must not be touched, so no spherical component is prescribed. A cylindrical lens has power in one meridian and none in the meridian at right angles to it, which is precisely the correction required.\n\nThe lecture's lens table assigns the three lens shapes to the three jobs (L7,8 p32):\n\n| Lens | Corrects |\n|---|---|\n| Concave / minus (spherical) | Myopia |\n| Convex / plus (spherical) | Hyperopia and presbyopia |\n| **Cylindrical** | **Astigmatism** |\n\n**B, spherical lenses only**, would move *both* meridians by the same amount. Applied to simple astigmatism, that spoils the meridian that was already correct while only partly helping the other — the interval between the two focal lines (the interval of Sturm) is unchanged, because a sphere shifts the whole interval without shortening it.\n\n**C, A & B together**, is the correct answer to the *next* version of this question, not to this one. A spherocylinder is what compound and mixed astigmatism need, where both meridians are ametropic and so both a shift and a collapse of the interval are required — that is Q22 of this chapter, keyed C. The bank prints the two questions with identical option lists precisely so that the distinction has to be made rather than guessed.\n\n**D, none of the above**, is excluded once A is established.\n\nA practical caveat the deck does not raise: what is dispensed in a spectacle prescription is often written as a sphere plus a cylinder even in simple astigmatism, because plus-cylinder and minus-cylinder forms of the same lens transpose into each other. The optical *requirement*, which is what the question tests, is still cylindrical power alone. *(Transposition is explicitly absent from the course material — the deck draws both plus and minus cylinders on L7,8 p34 and never gives the transposition rule.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Simple astigmatism has one emmetropic meridian, so a cylinder alone corrects it; compound and mixed types need a spherocylindrical lens.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.16 (Ch.2 Q3)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding myopia?",
    options:["It often stops progression in late 2nd or early 3rd decade","It causes blurred distant vision","It might be complicated by posterior staphyloma","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three statements are printed in the lecture, so D is the key.** This is a straight recall question assembled from three separate myopia slides.\n\n**A** — \"Often stops progression in the **late 2nd decade or the early years of the 3rd decade**\" (L7,8 p43), which also records that myopia \"usually presents and progresses in the 1st or 2nd decades of life\".\n\n**B** — the symptom list on the same slide gives \"Blurred distance vision\", adding that \"Near vision is usually not affected **except in higher degrees of myopia**\" (L7,8 p43). This follows from the definition: in myopia parallel rays focus **in front of** the retina with accommodation at rest, and the far point lies in front of the patient rather than at infinity (L7,8 p39, p41).\n\n**C** — **posterior staphyloma** is listed as a complication in its own right on the myopia complications tree (L7,8 p46), alongside chorioretinal degenerations, complicated cataract and a high association with primary open-angle glaucoma. A staphyloma is an ectatic bulge of the thinned posterior sclera lined by uvea, and it is the structural consequence of the same axial elongation that caused the myopia.\n\n**E, none of the above**, is excluded by any one of the three.\n\nA note on the corpus: `opqb-t2-112` in Grade Gain asks which staphyloma is most common in myopia and keys **posterior**, from the same slide.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Myopia presents in the first two decades and usually arrests in the late 2nd/early 3rd, blurs distance vision, and may be complicated by posterior staphyloma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.16 (Ch.2 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"A 34-year-old man complains of blurred near vision. Which of the following is the most likely cause of that patient's condition?",
    options:["Presbyopia","Hyperopia","Myopia","Astigmatism"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**At 34, blurred near vision with no other complaint is uncorrected hyperopia declaring itself — the accommodation that had been hiding it is running out, and this is a decade too early for presbyopia.**\n\nThe lecture stages hyperopia by decade, and this patient sits exactly on the hinge (L7,8 p55, p56):\n\n| Age band | What the hyperope complains of |\n|---|---|\n| 1st–3rd decades | Usually no glasses needed for distance (accommodation compensates); asthenopia with prolonged near work; uncommonly accommodative esotropia in childhood |\n| **4th and 5th decades** | **Blurred near vision, may need reading glasses earlier than usual (early presbyopia)** |\n| Above 50 | Both distance and near blurred, more for near |\n\nA 34-year-old is in the fourth decade, and the fourth-decade cell is *blurred near vision* — the stem's only symptom. The mechanism is on L7,8 p54: \"Accommodation by the ciliary muscle can correct hypermetropia in younger patients but this ability decreases with age as the lens elasticity becomes less.\" The hyperope has been spending accommodation continuously just to see clearly, so as amplitude falls the near task is the first to fail.\n\n**A, presbyopia**, is the trap and it is close, because presbyopia produces the identical symptom. It is excluded on age: the deck defines presbyopia as \"a **normal aging process** (usually in persons **40 years of age or older**)\" (L7,8 p73), and gives the amplitude curve 14 D at age 10 → 3 D at age 40 → completely lost after 60 (L7,8 p72). At 34 an emmetrope still has enough amplitude in reserve. Note the deck's own hedge — the hyperope reaches near-vision trouble \"earlier than usual (**early presbyopia**)\" — so the two answers are not unrelated: the hyperope is presbyopic early *because* he is hyperopic, and hyperopia is the cause the question asks for.\n\n**C, myopia**, is the wrong direction entirely. Myopia focuses rays in front of the retina and blurs **distance** vision; near vision is preserved except in high degrees (L7,8 p43). A myope of this age typically reads *better* than an emmetrope without glasses.\n\n**D, astigmatism**, blurs vision at all distances rather than near alone, and its higher degrees present with a package the stem does not describe — narrowing of the palpebral fissure, asthenopia and headaches; mild degrees are asymptomatic (L7,8 p68). Isolated near blur is not its signature.\n\nThis stem is the antecedent for Q6 and Q7 of this chapter, both of which say \"the previous case\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Uncorrected hyperopia declares itself as blurred near vision in the 4th–5th decades, as failing accommodation stops compensating — earlier than true presbyopia, which starts at about 40.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.17 (Ch.2 Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"For the 34-year-old man in the previous case complaining of blurred near vision, all of the following lines of treatment may be employed to correct the patient's refractive error except?",
    options:["Spherical concave lens glasses","Spherical convex lens glasses","Contact lenses","LASIK surgery"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Concave (minus) glasses are the myope's lens, and this patient is hyperopic — a minus lens would push his focus further behind the retina and make him worse.** Every other option is on the lecture's hyperopia treatment tree.\n\nThe lens assignment is categorical (L7,8 p32, p33):\n\n| Lens | Effect on light | Prescribed for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Concave / minus** | **Diverges** rays | **Myopia** |\n| Convex / plus | Converges rays | Hyperopia and presbyopia |\n| Cylindrical | Power in one meridian | Astigmatism |\n\nThe hyperopic eye already focuses parallel rays **behind** the retina (L7,8 p49). It needs extra convergence, so **B, spherical convex lens glasses**, is the correct treatment — the lecture's tree gives \"Glasses — **Convex or plus lenses**\" as the first conservative option (L7,8 p59), and its figure shows a plus lens bringing the focus forward onto the retina (L7,8 p60).\n\n**C, contact lenses**, is the second conservative arm of the same tree (L7,8 p59). The deck's one general statement on them: contact lenses give \"Cosmetic & a wider field of vision however they are associated with **risk of infection**\" (L7,8 p35).\n\n**D, LASIK surgery**, is the corneal surgical option for hyperopia (L7,8 p59). ⚠️ Worth noting because the deck is asymmetric here and gives no reason: myopia is offered PRK, LASIK, phakic IOL under 40 and clear lens extraction over 40 (L7,8 p47), whereas hyperopia is offered **only** LASIK and clear lens extraction — no PRK and no age cut-off (L7,8 p59). LASIK is on both lists, so the option is safe either way.\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED.** The bank prints only \"In the previous case\"; the antecedent is Q5 of this chapter — the 34-year-old man with blurred near vision — and the stem above restates him by his presentation. His *diagnosis* is deliberately not restated, because it is the key to Q5.\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE MISPRINT REPAIRED.** The bank prints \"the patient's **refracrtive** error\"; the option text and stem above give \"refractive\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hyperopia is corrected with convex (plus) lenses, contact lenses or refractive surgery; concave minus lenses correct myopia and would worsen a hyperope.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.17 (Ch.2 Q6)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"For the same 34-year-old man complaining of blurred near vision, which of the following is NOT among the complications that might arise in that patient?",
    options:["Squint (accommodative esotropia)","Narrow angle glaucoma","Amblyopia","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Amblyopia cannot now arise in a 34-year-old: it is a failure of visual development and the developmental window closed decades ago.** The other two options are the lecture's own hyperopia complications and remain live risks at this age.\n\nThe complications slide lists exactly two headings (L7,8 p58):\n\n| Complication | Mechanism as printed |\n|---|---|\n| **Narrow angle glaucoma** | \"Predisposition to narrow angle glaucoma due to **shallow anterior chamber** (especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker)\" |\n| **Squint** | \"Apparent divergent squint\" and \"**Accommodative esotropia**\" |\n\n**B, narrow angle glaucoma**, is not merely still possible at 34 — the deck says the risk *grows*, \"especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker\". A short eye has a crowded anterior segment, and a lens that thickens with age pushes the iris-lens diaphragm forward, narrowing an already shallow angle. This man is moving toward that risk, not away from it.\n\n**A, accommodative esotropia**, is the one that needs care. The deck ties it to childhood — hyperopes \"uncommonly develop accommodative esotropia in the **childhood period**\" (L7,8 p55) — so it might look as excluded by age as amblyopia is. It is not, and the difference is the mechanism. Accommodative esotropia is driven by the accommodation-convergence link: sustained accommodative effort drags convergence with it. That link works at any age at which accommodation is still being exerted, and a 34-year-old is still exerting it (he is symptomatic precisely because he is running out). Adult-onset accommodative esotropia is uncommon but real. Amblyopia has no such adult route.\n\n**C, amblyopia**, is therefore the answer. Amblyopia is defective vision in a structurally normal eye caused by abnormal visual input **during the critical period of visual development**, roughly the first seven to eight years of life. After that period the cortical connections are set: a newly blurred image in an adult eye produces blur that clears the moment it is corrected, not amblyopia. The chapter agrees with itself here — Q16 lists amblyopia as a hyperopia complication but qualifies it \"**if it develops in young children**\", and the qualifier is doing exactly the work this question tests. *(The critical-period account of amblyopia is not taken from the course material: the deck names amblyopia only once, on L7,8 p83 as a consequence of anisometropia, and never defines, classifies or ages it — its own NOT-COVERED list records \"no occlusion therapy, no critical period, no age limit\".)*\n\n**D, none of the above**, would require all three to be possible; C is not.\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED, AND IT IS TWO LINKS DEEP.** The bank prints \"In the previous case\", but the literally previous item, Q6, is itself a back-reference and contains no patient. The patient is Q5's 34-year-old man with blurred near vision, and \"that patient\" later in the same sentence points at him. The stem above restates him by presentation only — his diagnosis is Q5's key and is not repeated here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hyperopia risks narrow-angle glaucoma and accommodative esotropia at any age, but amblyopia can only arise within the critical period of visual development in childhood.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.17 (Ch.2 Q7)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"A 22-year-old male complains of frequent headaches and blurred vision. Eye examination revealed that the corneal surface is NOT spherical. What's the diagnosis of that condition?",
    options:["Myopia","Hypermetropia","Lenticular astigmatism","Corneal astigmatism"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A non-spherical cornea is corneal astigmatism by definition, and the headache is its classic accompaniment.** The lecture's definition names both the surface and the site: astigmatism \"is due non-spherical surface of the **cornea (more common)** or the **lens**\" with a resulting difference in refractive power \"at **different meridians**\" (L7,8 p61) — the cornea or lens surface having different radii of curvature instead of being part of a sphere with a single radius.\n\nThe symptoms fit the higher-degree presentation (L7,8 p68): blurred vision, narrowing of the palpebral fissure to overcome the defective vision, asthenopia, and **headaches**. Mild degrees are asymptomatic and pass unnoticed, so a symptomatic 22-year-old is being described as having more than a trivial cylinder.\n\n**A, myopia**, and **B, hypermetropia**, are both spherical errors of a spherical surface. They can cause blurring, and hyperopia can certainly cause headache through asthenopia, but neither is what \"the corneal surface is NOT spherical\" describes. The stem hands over the examination finding that separates them.\n\n**C, lenticular astigmatism**, is the right family and the wrong site. The deck is explicit on the relative frequency: on its causes tree, \"**Lenticular Astigmatism (very rare)**\" against \"**Corneal Astigmatism (most common)**\" (L7,8 p64) — and lenticular astigmatism would be found by examining the lens, not the corneal surface. The stem locates the abnormality on the cornea, which decides between C and D.\n\n**D** is therefore the key on two independent grounds: the site the stem gives, and the frequency the deck prints.\n\nThis stem is the antecedent for Q9 and Q10 of this chapter, both of which say \"the previous case\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A cornea whose surface is not spherical has different power in different meridians — corneal astigmatism, the commonest form; lenticular astigmatism is very rare.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.17 (Ch.2 Q8)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"What is the prevalence of corneal astigmatism, the condition described in the previous case?",
    options:["10%","20%","30%","40%"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The bank keys 30 %.** ⚠️ **This figure is not in the course material, and the absence was measured rather than assumed.** The cached lecture carries a complete inventory of every numeric value printed on all 88 of its slides, and it contains no percentage at all; the deck's NOT-COVERED register states plainly that it \"contains **no percentage and no prevalence figure of any kind**, for any refractive error, anywhere\" (L7,8, deck-wide, verified visually over all 88 pages plus a full-text grep of three extraction modes). No book chapter on refraction exists in the cached set either — the ophthalmology book caches run examination, orbit, lacrimal, lids, conjunctiva, sclera, uvea, neuro-ophthalmology, squint, paediatric and tumours, with no refraction chapter among them.\n\nSo the figure has to be filled from general knowledge, and it holds up. *(The following is not taken from the course material.)* Population surveys of adults consistently put astigmatism of at least 0.5 dioptre in roughly **a third of eyes** — pooled adult estimates cluster around 30–40 %, rising with age as the cornea drifts from with-the-rule toward against-the-rule. Almost all of it is **corneal** rather than lenticular, which is why a prevalence quoted for \"astigmatism\" is effectively a prevalence for corneal astigmatism. Set the threshold higher, at a clinically significant 1.0 D or more, and the figure falls to roughly 15–20 %; set it at any measurable cylinder and it approaches everyone, since a perfectly spherical cornea is a textbook idealisation. **A quoted prevalence for astigmatism is meaningless without the dioptric threshold it was measured at**, and the bank prints no threshold. 30 % is the answer that matches the usual ≥0.5 D convention.\n\n**A, 10 %**, and **B, 20 %**, undershoot at that threshold — 20 % is closer to the ≥1.0 D figure, not the ≥0.5 D one. **D, 40 %**, is at the top of the reported range rather than its centre, and is the figure this same chapter attaches to *myopia* in Q24's keyed option (\"around 30-40% of the population\"). ⚠️ The bank therefore prints overlapping prevalence ranges for two different refractive errors in one chapter and never reconciles them; both are recorded as printed, and neither key is disputed.\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED.** \"The previous case\" is Q8 of this chapter — the 22-year-old with a non-spherical corneal surface, keyed corneal astigmatism. Naming the condition here does not leak anything: Q9 cannot be answered without knowing what condition is being counted, and Q8's key is already spent by the time this question is reached in the printed order.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The bank puts the prevalence of corneal astigmatism at 30 %; population figures for astigmatism ≥0.5 D cluster around 30–40 %, and any such figure depends entirely on the dioptric threshold used.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.17 (Ch.2 Q9)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"What is the type of glasses used to treat patients with corneal astigmatism, the condition described in the case above?",
    options:["Minus lenses","Plus lenses","Cylindrical lenses","Reading glasses"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Astigmatism is corrected with cylindrical lenses — the lecture assigns them to nothing else.** Its lens tree (L7,8 p32) reads:\n\n| Lens | Corrects |\n|---|---|\n| Concave / **minus** (spherical) | Myopia |\n| Convex / **plus** (spherical) | Hyperopia **and presbyopia** |\n| **Cylindrical** | **Astigmatism** |\n\nAnd the astigmatism treatment tree confirms it from the other side: the conservative arm for regular astigmatism is \"**Glasses (Cylindrical lenses)**\" and soft toric contact lenses (L7,8 p71).\n\nThe optical reason is the geometry the stem already gave in Q8. A non-spherical cornea has different power in its two principal meridians, so light is brought to two focal lines rather than one point. A spherical lens changes both meridians equally and cannot close that gap; a cylinder has power in one meridian and none at right angles to it, so it can add power to the weaker meridian alone and collapse the two focal lines into a point.\n\n**A, minus lenses**, diverge light and are the myope's correction (L7,8 p33: concave lenses \"**Diverge light rays**\", object smaller and moves with). **B, plus lenses**, converge light and belong to hyperopia and presbyopia (L7,8 p33). Either one alone leaves the astigmatic interval intact.\n\n**D, reading glasses**, are a presbyopic correction — a spherical plus addition to the distance prescription (L7,8 p81) — and address a near-vision problem this 22-year-old does not have. His blur is at all distances, not confined to near.\n\n**BACK-REFERENCE REPAIRED, AND IT IS TWO LINKS DEEP.** The bank prints \"the condition mentioned in the previous case\", but the literally previous item, Q9, is itself a back-reference to Q8 and describes no case. The condition is Q8's corneal astigmatism; the stem above names it, which is necessary for the question to stand alone and gives nothing away that Q8 and Q9 have not already spent.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Cylindrical lenses correct astigmatism by adding power in one meridian only; minus lenses are for myopia, plus lenses for hyperopia and presbyopia.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.18 (Ch.2 Q10)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Amblyopia occurs commonly with which of the following refractive errors?",
    options:["Low myopia","High myopia","Low hyperopia","High hyperopia"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**High hyperopia is the refractive error most likely to cause amblyopia, because the hyperopic child never gets a clear image at any distance and cannot escape the blur.**\n\n⚠️ **The course material does not answer this question, and the absence was measured.** The refraction deck names amblyopia exactly once — \"[anisometropia] is the most common cause of amblyopia in children\" (L7,8 p83) — and its NOT-COVERED register states that amblyopia is \"never defined, classified, or treated. No occlusion therapy, no critical period, no age limit\". It never ranks refractive errors by amblyogenic risk. What follows is filled from general medical knowledge and is *not taken from the course material*.\n\nA refractive error causes amblyopia only if it degrades the retinal image **during the critical period of visual development**. That gives two conditions: the blur must be substantial, and the child must be unable to overcome it.\n\n**D, high hyperopia**, meets both. A hyperopic child can accommodate, but accommodating enough to clear a large hyperopic error means accommodating maximally and continuously, which is unsustainable; and whatever accommodation he exerts pulls convergence with it, so the common outcome is accommodative esotropia — which the lecture does list as a hyperopia complication (L7,8 p55, p58) — and esotropia carries its own strabismic amblyopia on top of the refractive one. Bilateral high hyperopia is the classic cause of **isometropic ametropic amblyopia**; the usual quoted threshold is above roughly +5 D, well below the equivalent myopic threshold.\n\n**B, high myopia**, is the intended near-miss and is genuinely amblyogenic, but less so. The high myope has a near point very close to the eye — the lecture makes this point in its own words: a person with myopia \"has a near point that is **closer to the eye than normal**\" (L7,8 p72). He therefore sees small objects held close with perfect clarity, and the developing visual cortex receives a sharp foveal image for part of every day. The threshold for myopic ametropic amblyopia is correspondingly much higher, usually quoted above roughly −8 D.\n\n**A, low myopia**, and **C, low hyperopia**, are not amblyogenic at all in the ordinary case. Low hyperopia is fully neutralised by accommodation in a child — the lecture says young hyperopes \"usually do not require glasses for correction of far vision (as hyperopia is compensated by accommodation)\" (L7,8 p55) — and low myopia leaves near vision sharp.\n\nOne caveat that reconciles this with Q13: the greatest amblyogenic risk of all is **anisometropia**, a difference *between* the eyes, and the deck says so (L7,8 p83). This question asks which single refractive error is commonly amblyogenic, not which cause is commonest overall; Q13 asks the second question and keys anisometropia.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"High hyperopia is the most amblyogenic isolated refractive error — the child cannot sustain the accommodation needed to clear it and risks accommodative esotropia; high myopia spares near vision and is less amblyogenic.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.18 (Ch.2 Q11)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"All of the following conditions can contribute to the occurrence of hyperopia except?",
    options:["Increased refractive index of the lens","Short axial length of the eye","Aphakia","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**An increased refractive index of the lens makes the eye more powerful, which is index MYOPIA — the exact opposite of hyperopia.** The two causes slides are mirror images and the question is built on the mirror.\n\n| | Myopia (L7,8 p42) | Hyperopia (L7,8 p53) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Axial | AL **longer** than normal (22-24.5 mm) | AL **shorter** than normal (22-24.5 mm) |\n| Curvature | **Increased** corneal curvature (keratoconus), lens (lenticonus) | **Decreased** corneal curvature (corneal flattening) |\n| Index | **Increase** refractive index of cornea; **increase** refractive index of the lens nucleus (**nuclear cataract**) | **Decrease** refractive index of lens; **aphakia** (absence of the lens) or posterior dislocation of the lens |\n\n**A** is lifted verbatim from the *myopia* column. A denser lens nucleus refracts more strongly, the eye's total power exceeds what its axial length needs, and rays focus in front of the retina. The lecture's clinical example is nuclear cataract, which is why an elderly patient with a nuclear sclerotic lens may find he can read without his glasses again — the so-called second sight. So A is a real cause of ametropia, just the wrong one, and that is what makes it the answer to an EXCEPT question.\n\n**B, short axial length**, is the first-listed cause of hyperopia and the commonest one — the deck calls axial length the \"main factor usually\" in determining any state of refraction (L7,8 p25), and its comparative figure draws the hyperopic globe with the visibly shortest axial-length arrow (L7,8 p29). Rays reach the retina before they have converged.\n\n**C, aphakia**, is printed under index hypermetropia on the hyperopia slide (L7,8 p53). Removing the lens removes 18–20 dioptres from a system that totals about 60 (L7,8 p23) — a third of the eye's power gone — leaving a grossly underpowered eye that focuses far behind the retina. Q18 of this chapter tests the same fact as a clinical vignette.\n\n**D, none of the above**, would require all three to cause hyperopia; A does not.\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE MISPRINT REPAIRED.** The bank prints \"the **occurence** of hyperopia\"; the stem above gives \"occurrence\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hyperopia comes from a short axial length, a flatter cornea, or a lens of reduced power including aphakia; an increased lens refractive index causes index myopia instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.18 (Ch.2 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of amblyopia in children?",
    options:["Strabismus","Anisometropia","Occlusion amblyopia","Bilateral uncorrected refractive errors"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The lecture states this outright: anisometropia \"is the most common cause of amblyopia in children\" (L7,8 p83).** No inference is needed — the sentence is printed on the anisometropia slide and is the deck's only statement about amblyopia anywhere.\n\nAnisometropia is \"a difference in the refractive status between both eyes\" (L7,8 p83). Its danger is that it is silent: the better eye sees normally, so the child has no complaint, the parents see nothing wrong, and there is no squint to draw attention. The blurred eye is quietly suppressed through the whole critical period and the loss is only found at a school screening or a routine examination years later. The deck adds the practical consequence: a small difference is corrected with glasses, but a large one — \"usually more than **4 D** difference\" — may induce **diplopia** through **aniseikonia**, unequal retinal image size, which is \"an obstacle to fusion\" (L7,8 p83, p84).\n\n**A, strabismus**, is the second commonest and is the one most people name first, because it is the visible one. A constantly deviating eye is suppressed to avoid diplopia and becomes amblyopic. It loses to anisometropia on frequency for the same reason it comes to mind first: it is obvious, so it is caught and treated, whereas anisometropia is not.\n\n**C, occlusion amblyopia**, is iatrogenic — amblyopia of the *good* eye caused by patching it too hard or too long while treating the fellow eye. It is a recognised complication of amblyopia therapy and is uncommon by definition, since it can only occur in a child already under treatment. *(Occlusion amblyopia is not taken from the course material; the deck covers no amblyopia treatment at all.)*\n\n**D, bilateral uncorrected refractive errors**, causes isometropic ametropic amblyopia — the mechanism behind Q11 of this chapter, where high hyperopia is keyed. It is real but much rarer than anisometropia, because it requires a large error in *both* eyes rather than a difference between them, and a large bilateral error is more likely to produce a complaint that brings the child in.\n\nThe two questions are consistent: Q11 asks which single refractive error is commonly amblyogenic (high hyperopia), and this one asks which cause is commonest overall (anisometropia, a difference between the eyes rather than an error in one).\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE MISPRINT REPAIRED.** The bank prints option C as \"Occlusion **ambylopia**\"; the option above gives \"amblyopia\", which is how the same word is spelled in this stem and in Q11.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Anisometropia is the commonest cause of childhood amblyopia because it is asymptomatic and unnoticed; strabismic amblyopia is second, and bilateral ametropic and occlusion amblyopia are rarer.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.18 (Ch.2 Q13)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"In hypermetropia, blurring of both distant and near vision occurs in which of the following age groups?",
    options:["Young age","Middle age","Old age","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Old age — the lecture's third band, above 50, is the only one in which the hyperope's distance vision fails as well as his near.** The three-band presentation is printed across two slides and reading it in order answers the question directly (L7,8 p55, p56):\n\n| Band | What fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Young age** — 1st, 2nd, 3rd decades | Neither, for distance: \"usually do not require glasses for correction of far vision (as hyperopia is compensated by accommodation)\". May get asthenopia with prolonged near work; uncommonly accommodative esotropia in childhood |\n| **Middle age** — 4th and 5th decades | Near only: \"blurred near vision and may require near vision correction (reading glasses) **earlier than usual (early presbyopia)**\" |\n| **Old age** — above 50 | **Both**: \"Blurring of **both distance and near vision (more for near)** due to markedly reduced amplitude of accommodation\" |\n\nThe single mechanism running through all three bands is the accommodative reserve. The hyperopic eye is underpowered, so it must accommodate even to see at distance; whether it succeeds depends on how much amplitude is left. The deck's amplitude figures make the collapse concrete: 14 dioptres at age 10, 3 dioptres by 40, completely lost after 60 (L7,8 p72).\n\n**A, young age**, is wrong because at full amplitude the error is entirely hidden for distance. This is the point of the deck's caution that hyperopia is *latent* in the young; the eye pays for clear vision with continuous effort, and the price shows as asthenopia, not as blur.\n\n**B, middle age**, is wrong because it is the band where near alone gives way. Near work demands the largest accommodative effort — the hyperopic error plus the near demand on top of it — so near is always the first casualty. This is exactly the patient in Q5 of this chapter.\n\n**D, all of the above**, is excluded by the young and middle bands.\n\nThe order matters clinically: **near before distance** is the signature of hyperopia. The reverse order — distance failing while near stays clear — is myopia (L7,8 p43), which is Q19 of this chapter.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The hyperope loses near vision in the 4th–5th decades and both near and distance above 50, as accommodative amplitude falls from 14 D at age 10 to 3 D at 40 and none after 60.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.18 (Ch.2 Q14)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"In myopia, the near point is situated in which of the following sites?",
    options:["Closer to the eye than normal","Further from the eye than normal","Behind the eye","Normal location"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Printed verbatim in the lecture: \"A person with **myopia** has a near point that is **closer to the eye than normal**\" (L7,8 p72).**\n\nThe near point is defined on the same slide as \"the nearest point at which an object can be placed in front of the eye and still form a focused image on the retina\". The reason the myope's is closer follows from what myopia is: the eye is over-powered for its length, so with accommodation at rest it is already focused on something nearer than infinity. Every dioptre of accommodation it then adds brings the focus in from a starting point that was already close, so the nearest point it can reach is closer than an emmetrope's.\n\nThe same slide gives the mirror statement for the other error: \"A person with **hyperopia** has a near point that is **farther from the eye than normal**\" — which is **B**, and is why the hyperope struggles at near before he struggles at distance (Q5, Q14 of this chapter).\n\n**C, behind the eye**, confuses the near point with the **far point**. It is the *far* point that lies behind the eye, and only in hyperopia: the deck's footers set them out one error at a time — emmetropia, \"Far point at infinity\" (L7,8 p18); myopia, \"Far point **in front of the patient**\" (L7,8 p39); hyperopia, \"Far point **behind the eye**\" (L7,8 p49). A point behind the eye is virtual, which is exactly why the hyperopic eye cannot focus at rest without help.\n\n**D, normal location**, describes the emmetrope. It is also worth noting that a normally located near point is not the same as normal near vision: the deck's presbyopia table shows the low myope (up to 4–5 D) *exploiting* his close near point by taking his glasses off to read, whereas the high myope wearing his glasses behaves \"as emmetropes\" (L7,8 p75) — the glasses neutralise the myopia, and the near point moves back with it.\n\nThis is the optical fact behind a common clinical observation: the presbyopic low myope who reads happily without spectacles, and the presbyopic hyperope who needs reading glasses years early.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The myope's near point is closer to the eye than normal and the hyperope's is further; a far point behind the eye is the hallmark of hyperopia, not a near point.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.19 (Ch.2 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is not among the complications of hyperopia?",
    options:["Squint","Amblyopia if it develops in young children","Narrow angle glaucoma","Open angle glaucoma","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Open-angle glaucoma belongs to myopia, not hyperopia — and the lecture prints both associations on separate slides, so the two can be compared directly.**\n\nHyperopia's complications (L7,8 p58): \"Predisposition to **narrow angle glaucoma** due to shallow anterior chamber (especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker)\" and \"**Squint** — apparent divergent squint, accommodative esotropia\". Myopia's complications (L7,8 p46): chorioretinal degenerations, posterior staphyloma, complicated cataract, and \"**High association with primary open angle Glaucoma**\".\n\nThe anatomy explains the split cleanly. The hyperopic eye is **short**, so its anterior segment is crowded and its anterior chamber shallow; the iris sits close to the trabecular meshwork and the angle is narrow, which is the setup for angle closure. Ageing worsens it, as the deck notes, because the lens thickens and pushes the iris-lens diaphragm further forward. The myopic eye is **long**, with a deep anterior chamber and a wide open angle — angle closure is essentially not its risk. Its glaucoma is open-angle, associated instead with the structural weakness of a stretched globe and a large, susceptible optic disc.\n\n**A, squint**, is printed on the hyperopia slide in both its forms. **B, amblyopia if it develops in young children**, is correct with its qualifier attached — and the qualifier is doing real work, because it is what makes amblyopia the *wrong* answer here while making it the *right* answer in Q7, where the patient is 34. Amblyopia requires the critical period of visual development; the deck names amblyopia only as a consequence of anisometropia (L7,8 p83) and never sets an age limit, but the bank supplies the limit itself in this option.\n\n**C, narrow angle glaucoma**, is the hyperopia association verbatim.\n\n**E, none of the above**, would require all four to be hyperopia complications; D is not.\n\nThe pair is worth memorising as a single line: **short eye, shallow chamber, closed angle; long eye, deep chamber, open angle.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hyperopia predisposes to narrow-angle glaucoma through a shallow anterior chamber, plus squint and childhood amblyopia; open-angle glaucoma is the association of high myopia.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.19 (Ch.2 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Which of the following is among the lines of management used to treat irregular astigmatism?",
    options:["RGP contact lenses","Soft toric contact lenses","ICR segments","A & C","B & C"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**RGP contact lenses and intracorneal ring segments are both on the irregular arm of the lecture's treatment tree; soft toric lenses are on the regular arm.** The tree splits before anything else, and the split is the whole question (L7,8 p71):\n\n| | **Regular astigmatism** | **Irregular astigmatism** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Conservative | Glasses (cylindrical lenses); **soft toric (sphero-cylindrical) CLs** | Glasses (mild degrees only); **rigid gas permeable (RGP) CLs** |\n| Surgical | Corneal surgery — LASIK, PRK; lenticular surgery | **Intracorneal ring (ICR) segments**; keratoplasty |\n\n**A, RGP contact lenses**, work in irregular astigmatism through a mechanism no spectacle lens can reproduce: the rigid lens vaults the distorted cornea and the tear film fills the gap between them, so the refracting surface becomes the lens's own regular front surface rather than the patient's irregular cornea. The irregularity is optically neutralised rather than measured and corrected. *(The tear-lens mechanism is not taken from the course material; the deck names RGP lenses without describing how they work.)*\n\n**C, ICR segments**, are the surgical option on the same arm — plastic segments implanted in the corneal stroma to flatten and regularise the ectatic cone. The deck lists them beside keratoplasty for irregular astigmatism (L7,8 p71).\n\n**B, soft toric contact lenses**, is the distractor and it is a good one, because toric lenses do correct astigmatism — but *regular* astigmatism, where the two principal meridians are at right angles and each can be given a defined power. A soft lens drapes onto the cornea and conforms to it, so it reproduces the irregularity underneath instead of masking it. That is why it appears on the regular arm and not the irregular one.\n\n**D** collects A and C and is therefore the key; **E** pairs the wrong lens with the right surgery.\n\nThe clinical driver behind this whole arm is keratoconus — the lecture names \"**Corneal scars and Keratoconus**\" as the causes of irregular astigmatism (L7,8 p64), which is also the subject of Q27 of this chapter. Note that glasses are *not* absent from the irregular arm, but they are qualified: \"Glasses (**Mild degrees**)\" only.\n\nA cross-bank note: `opqb-t2-81` in the corpus asks the same slide from the opposite direction — \"Irregular astigmatism is NOT corrected by:\", keyed *glasses*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Irregular astigmatism is managed with RGP contact lenses, which mask the irregularity under a tear lens, and surgically with ICR segments or keratoplasty; soft toric lenses treat regular astigmatism only.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.19 (Ch.2 Q17)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"A 52-year-old male patient underwent a cataract surgery during which the lens was removed and not replaced by an artificial one. What is the expected refractive error that will develop in such a patient after the operation?",
    options:["Myopia","Hypermetropia","Astigmatism","Presbyopia"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Removing the lens and putting nothing back leaves an aphakic eye, and aphakia is high hypermetropia — the lecture files it under index hypermetropia by name: \"**Aphakia** (Absence of the lens) or posterior dislocation of the lens\" (L7,8 p53).**\n\nThe arithmetic is on the optics slide. The eye's total power is about **60 dioptres**, of which the cornea supplies **42–44 D** and the lens **18–20 D** (L7,8 p23). Take the lens out and roughly a third of the refracting power goes with it, while the axial length is unchanged. A grossly underpowered eye of normal length focuses parallel rays far behind the retina — the definition of hypermetropia (L7,8 p49) — and the resulting error is large, of the order of +10 D or more at the spectacle plane. *(The magnitude figure is not taken from the course material; the deck names aphakia as a cause and gives no dioptric value for it.)*\n\n**A, myopia**, is the reverse. Index *myopia* is caused by an **increase** in the refractive index of the lens nucleus — nuclear cataract (L7,8 p42) — which is what the patient probably had **before** surgery. That is the trap: the cataract itself may well have made him more myopic, and removing it swings him hard the other way.\n\n**C, astigmatism**, can certainly follow cataract surgery, from the wound and from suturing, and modern surgery is designed to minimise it. But it is a surgical side-effect of variable size, not the refractive error that *removing the lens* predictably produces. The question asks what the absent lens does.\n\n**D, presbyopia**, is a real feature of the aphakic eye — with no lens there is nothing to accommodate, so the loss of accommodation is total rather than age-related — but it is not the refractive error. Presbyopia is defined as recession of the near point with age (L7,8 p73), and at 52 this man was presbyopic before the operation anyway (the deck puts presbyopia at 40 years or older, L7,8 p73). It adds nothing that the surgery caused.\n\nThe modern answer to all of this is the intraocular lens implanted at the time of surgery, which replaces the missing 18–20 D inside the eye. The stem specifies that this was not done, which is what makes the question about aphakia rather than pseudophakia. *(Pseudophakia and IOL power calculation are explicitly absent from the course material — the deck's NOT-COVERED register records that they are \"not mentioned at all\".)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Aphakia removes the lens's 18–20 D from a ~60 D eye of unchanged length, producing high hypermetropia; nuclear cataract does the opposite and causes index myopia.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.19 (Ch.2 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"A 15-year-old boy complains of blurry far vision with intact near vision. What is the expected refractive change that causes that condition?",
    options:["Increased dioptric power of the eye above normal","Decreased dioptric power of the eye below normal","Short axial length of the globe","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Blurred distance with clear near vision in a teenager is myopia, and myopia is an eye whose dioptric power is too high for its length — rays are brought to a focus in front of the retina.**\n\nThe presentation is the lecture's myopia slide almost word for word (L7,8 p43): myopia \"usually presents and progresses in the **1st or 2nd decades of life**\", and its symptoms are \"blurred distance vision\" with \"near vision usually not affected except in higher degrees\". A 15-year-old is squarely in the second decade.\n\nThe definition supplies the optics: myopia is the state in which parallel rays \"are focused **IN FRONT OF** the retina while accommodation is at rest\", with the far point in front of the patient rather than at infinity (L7,8 p39, p41). Focusing short of the retina means the eye is refracting *too strongly* for the distance available — an excess of power relative to length. That is **A**.\n\n**B** is the opposite, and it describes hyperopia: too little power for the length, rays focused behind the retina (L7,8 p49). It would blur near vision first, not distance.\n\n**C, short axial length of the globe**, is also hyperopia. It is the most tempting distractor because axial length is the deck's \"main factor usually\" in setting any refraction (L7,8 p25), and myopia *is* usually axial — but axial myopia is a globe that is too **long**, not too short: \"AL is longer than normal (22-24.5 mm)\" (L7,8 p42), and the comparative figure draws the myopic eye with the longest arrow of the three (L7,8 p29).\n\nThis raises the one nuance in the question, worth stating because it is where the sharper student hesitates. Refraction is set by the *relation* between the eye's power and its axial length, and the deck names both determinants: \"axial length of the eyeball (main factor usually)\" and \"power of the cornea and crystalline lens\" (L7,8 p25). Myopia can arise from either — axial myopia from a long globe, refractive myopia from excess corneal or lenticular power, whether by curvature (keratoconus, lenticonus) or by index (nuclear cataract) (L7,8 p42). Option A speaks of *dioptric power above normal*, which strictly names only the refractive route. It is nonetheless the key, and correctly so: with axial length not offered in the myopic direction, A is the only option that places the focus in front of the retina, and \"the eye's power is too high for it\" is a fair statement of what myopia is optically, whichever anatomical route produced it. **D, none of the above**, is therefore excluded.\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE MISPRINT REPAIRED.** The bank prints option B as \"Decreased dioptric power of the eye **above normal**\", which contradicts itself — a decrease cannot be above normal, and \"above normal\" belongs to option A alone. The option above reads \"below normal\", which is evidently what was intended and is what makes B the hyperopic mirror of A. The bank's printed wording is recorded here and the key is unaffected either way.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Myopia in the second decade blurs distance while sparing near, because the eye's refracting power is too high for its length and rays focus in front of the retina.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.19 (Ch.2 Q19)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"An 18-year-old girl was informed by the ophthalmologist that her eyesight was (-3) in her right eye and (-2.5) in her left eye. Which of the following accurately describes the girl's condition?",
    options:["It mostly arises due to increased axial length of the eye globe","It's characterized by clear near vision with blurred far vision","Both A & B","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A minus prescription is myopia, and both statements about myopia are true, so the key is C.**\n\nThe sign is the whole diagnosis. Minus lenses are concave, they diverge light, and the lecture assigns them to one error only: \"Concave (Minus Lenses) → **Myopia**\" (L7,8 p32, p33). A patient corrected with −3 and −2.5 dioptre lenses is a myope of those degrees — and by the deck's own cut-off both eyes are **simple myopia**, since simple myopia is \"less than 6 dioptres\" and high myopia can reach more than 20 (L7,8 p45).\n\n**A** is true. The deck names axial length \"the **main factor usually**\" determining any state of refraction (L7,8 p25), and axial myopia — \"AL is longer than normal (22-24.5 mm)\" — is the first-listed cause on the myopia causes slide, ahead of the refractive causes (L7,8 p42). Its comparative figure draws the myopic globe with the visibly longest axial arrow (L7,8 p29). The wording \"mostly\" is what makes A safe: refractive myopia exists, but it is the minority route.\n\n**B** is true and is the deck's symptom line: blurred distance vision, with near vision \"usually not affected **except in higher degrees of myopia**\" (L7,8 p43). At −3 and −2.5 she is nowhere near that exception, so her near vision is genuinely clear — indeed better than an emmetrope's at very close range, because her near point is closer to the eye than normal (L7,8 p72).\n\n**D, neither**, is excluded once either statement stands.\n\nOne feature of the vignette that is *not* being tested but is worth naming, because it looks like it might be: the two eyes differ by 0.5 D, which is anisometropia in the strict sense of \"a difference in the refractive status between both eyes\" (L7,8 p83). It is clinically irrelevant here. The deck's threshold for trouble is \"usually more than **4 D** difference\", above which spectacle correction can induce diplopia through aniseikonia (L7,8 p83); half a dioptre is far below that and is corrected with ordinary glasses without comment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A minus prescription means myopia, which is mostly axial (a long globe) and blurs distance while leaving near vision clear; a 0.5 D difference between eyes is trivial anisometropia.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.20 (Ch.2 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding presbyopia?",
    options:["It's caused by increased deformability of the lens that accompanies aging","It's characterized by reduced accommodative power that accompanies aging","By the age of 40, the accommodative eye power is lost totally","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Reduced accommodative power with age is one of the lecture's two printed causes of presbyopia; the other two options invert or overstate the deck's own figures.**\n\nThe causes slide gives exactly two lines (L7,8 p74):\n1. **Hardening (reduced deformability) of the lens with age**\n2. **Reduced accommodative amplitude with age**\n\n**B** is the second line restated, so it is true. The two causes are really one process seen from two sides — the lens stiffens, so it can no longer change shape, so the amplitude of accommodation falls, so the near point recedes. The deck defines presbyopia by that last consequence: \"**Recession of the near point** (i.e., it becomes farther from the eye) **with age**\", such that near images \"can no longer be appropriately focused on the retina using accommodation\" (L7,8 p73).\n\n**A** is the first cause with its sign reversed. The slide says \"**Hardening (reduced deformability)** of the lens\"; A says *increased* deformability. A more deformable lens would accommodate better, not worse. This is a one-word inversion and the commonest way this fact is mis-tested.\n\n**C** overstates the deck's numbers, and the deck's numbers are precise enough to refute it. The accommodative amplitude is \"**14 dioptres at the age of 10**\", diminishing \"to **3 dioptres by the age of 40**\" and \"**completely lost after the age of 60**\" (L7,8 p72). At 40 there are 3 dioptres left, not zero — which is the point of the age-40 threshold: 3 D is roughly what is needed to read at 33 cm, so 40 is the age at which the reserve stops being comfortable, not the age at which it disappears. Total loss comes two decades later. The deck's definition slide agrees, placing presbyopia in \"persons **40 years of age or older**\" (L7,8 p73) rather than declaring accommodation abolished there.\n\n**D, all of the above**, fails on A and C.\n\nNote also that presbyopia is not an error of the eye's optics at all in the sense the other three are — it is a normal ageing process (L7,8 p73), the far point does not move, and it is bilateral and symmetrical. Q29 of this chapter tests that same set of features from the other direction.\n\nA cross-bank note: `opqb-t2-72` in the corpus asks the definition — \"recession of the near point with ageing\" — and keys presbyopia.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Presbyopia is lens hardening with reduced accommodative amplitude — 14 D at age 10, 3 D at 40 and none after 60, so accommodation is diminished at 40, not abolished.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.20 (Ch.2 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Which of the following is the type of lenses used to correct compound OR mixed astigmatism?",
    options:["Cylindrical lenses only","Spherical lenses only","A & B together","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Compound and mixed astigmatism both have two ametropic meridians, so both need a sphere and a cylinder — a spherocylindrical lens.** This is the deliberate counterpart to Q3 of this chapter, which asks the same question about *simple* astigmatism and keys the cylinder alone.\n\nThe difference is in the definitions (L7,8 p64, p67):\n\n| Type | Meridian 1 | Meridian 2 | What the correction must do |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Simple** | emmetropic (focus on retina) | myopic **or** hyperopic | move one meridian only → **cylinder alone** |\n| **Compound** | both myopic, **or** both hyperopic | | move both, by different amounts → **sphere + cylinder** |\n| **Mixed** | one myopic | one hyperopic | move both, in opposite directions → **sphere + cylinder** |\n\nIn compound astigmatism both focal lines lie on the same side of the retina and neither is on it, so a spherical component is needed to shift the pair onto the retinal plane and a cylindrical component to close the gap between them. In mixed astigmatism the lines straddle the retina; the sphere shifts both, the cylinder collapses the interval, and no cylinder alone can do it because a cylinder moves only one meridian and here both are wrong.\n\nThe deck names the resulting lens where it lists contact lens options for regular astigmatism: \"Soft toric (**sphero-cylindrical**) CLs\" (L7,8 p71). The spectacle equivalent is written as a sphere with a cylinder at an axis.\n\n**A, cylindrical lenses only**, is the answer to Q3 and is wrong here for the reason set out above — it would leave the whole pair of focal lines displaced.\n\n**B, spherical lenses only**, is wrong for the opposite reason. A sphere moves both meridians equally, so it can shift the interval of Sturm but never shorten it; the patient is left with a residual cylinder however the sphere is chosen. The best a pure sphere can achieve is the spherical equivalent, a compromise focus that leaves both meridians blurred rather than either one clear. *(Spherical equivalent is explicitly absent from the course material — the deck's NOT-COVERED register lists it as \"not named, no formula\".)*\n\n**D, none of the above**, is excluded once C stands.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Compound and mixed astigmatism have two ametropic meridians and need a spherocylindrical lens; only simple astigmatism, with one emmetropic meridian, is corrected by a cylinder alone.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.20 (Ch.2 Q22)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is a known complication of high myopia?",
    options:["Diabetic retinopathy","Optic neuritis","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**RRD is on the lecture's myopia complications tree, reached through the peripheral chorioretinal degenerations (L7,8 p46).** The tree's true topology — recovered from the render, because the text layer had interleaved the columns — is that only the first branch subdivides:\n\n| Complications of myopia | |\n|---|---|\n| **Chorioretinal degenerations** — **Central** | myopic subfoveal CNV; macular haemorrhage and scarring |\n| **Chorioretinal degenerations** — **Peripheral** | night blindness; **retinal tears**; **RRD** |\n| Posterior staphyloma | |\n| Complicated cataract | |\n| High association with primary open angle glaucoma | |\n\nThe sequence is mechanical and is worth holding as a chain: the high myopic globe is elongated, so the retina is stretched thin at the periphery; thin retina degenerates; degenerate retina tears; a tear lets liquefied vitreous through into the subretinal space; the retina detaches. *Rhegmatogenous* means \"due to a break\", which names the third step of that chain. The deck's own list places retinal tears and RRD side by side under peripheral degeneration for exactly this reason. High myopia is the deck's progressive/degenerative/malignant type, reaching more than 20 dioptres (L7,8 p45).\n\n**A, diabetic retinopathy**, is a microvascular complication of diabetes mellitus and has no causal relation to axial length. If anything the association runs mildly the other way in clinical practice.\n\n**B, optic neuritis**, is inflammatory or demyelinating — classically associated with multiple sclerosis — and is not a mechanical consequence of a stretched globe. The myopic disc may look abnormal (tilted, with a temporal crescent), but that is an appearance, not neuritis.\n\n**D, central retinal artery occlusion**, is an embolic or thrombotic arterial event, a disease of carotid and cardiac sources and of giant cell arteritis, not of eye length.\n\nThe three wrong options share a feature worth naming: each is a *retinal or optic nerve* disease, so each looks plausible to a reader matching on organ rather than on mechanism. Only C follows from the anatomy that defines high myopia.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"High myopia stretches the peripheral retina into degeneration, tears and rhegmatogenous detachment; its other complications are posterior staphyloma, complicated cataract and an association with open-angle glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.20 (Ch.2 Q23)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding (near-sightedness)?",
    options:["It's also known as hyperopia","It's characterized by blurred near vision","It mostly occurs in old age","It happens in around 30-40% of the population"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Near-sightedness is myopia — the lecture prints the gloss itself, \"Myopia — Near-sightedness\" (L7,8 p31) — and of the four statements only the prevalence figure is true of it.**\n\n**A** is the direct contradiction. The same slide glosses \"Hyperopia — **Far**-sightedness\", so near-sighted and far-sighted are the two opposite errors, never synonyms. The names describe what the patient sees *well*: the near-sighted person sees near, the far-sighted person sees far.\n\n**B** inverts the symptom. Myopia blurs **distance** vision; near vision is usually unaffected except in higher degrees (L7,8 p43). Blurred near vision belongs to hyperopia in middle age (L7,8 p56) and to presbyopia (L7,8 p73). This is Q5 of this chapter, keyed hyperopia for that exact symptom.\n\n**C** inverts the age. Myopia \"usually presents and progresses in the **1st or 2nd decades of life**\" and \"often stops progression in the late 2nd decade or the early years of the 3rd\" (L7,8 p43) — it is a disease of schoolchildren and teenagers, and it has typically stabilised by the time the far-sighted patient is first becoming symptomatic. Old age brings presbyopia and index changes in the lens, not new simple myopia.\n\n**D** is the key by elimination and by figure. ⚠️ **The prevalence is not in the course material, and the absence was measured.** The cached lecture's complete inventory of every number printed on all 88 slides contains no percentage, and its NOT-COVERED register states the deck \"contains **no percentage and no prevalence figure of any kind**, for any refractive error, anywhere\". There is no refraction chapter among the cached book chapters either. *(What follows is not taken from the course material.)* Global prevalence of myopia in adults is usually quoted at roughly **30 %**, with wide geographic variation — under 20 % in parts of Africa and South America, but above 80 % in young adults in urban East Asia, and rising everywhere over the last three decades. The 30–40 % band in the option matches the commonly quoted worldwide figure.\n\n⚠️ **A DEFECT SHARED WITH Q9 OF THIS CHAPTER.** Q9 keys the prevalence of *corneal astigmatism* at 30 %, and this option gives *myopia* 30–40 %. The two ranges overlap, the bank prints neither a source nor a dioptric threshold for either, and it does not reconcile them. Both keys are recorded as printed and neither is disputed; the reader should treat both figures as the bank's, not as measured epidemiology.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Near-sightedness is myopia: it blurs distance not near, presents in the first two decades not old age, is the opposite of far-sightedness, and the bank puts its prevalence at 30–40 %.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.20 (Ch.2 Q24)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding the dioptric power of the eye?",
    options:["Cornea is the major contributor of eye dioptric power (42-44 dioptres)","Lens contributes by about 1/3 of eye dioptric power","The average dioptric power of eye is about 60 dioptres","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three numbers are printed on one slide, so D is the key.** The lecture's optics tree reads (L7,8 p23):\n\n| | Power |\n|---|---|\n| Cornea | **42–44 dioptres** |\n| Lens | **18–20 dioptres** |\n| **Total optical power** | **approximately 60 dioptres** |\n\n**A** and **C** are lifted from it directly. **B** requires one step of arithmetic: 18–20 out of about 60 is close to a third, so \"the lens contributes about 1/3\" is a correct paraphrase of the printed figures even though the deck never states the fraction itself. That leaves the cornea supplying roughly two thirds — which is why it is \"the major contributor\", the point Q32 of this chapter tests on its own.\n\nWhy the cornea outweighs the lens, when the lens looks like the more obvious optical element: refraction happens at an interface, and its strength depends on the *difference* in refractive index across that interface. The front surface of the cornea separates air from tissue, the largest index step anywhere in the eye. The lens sits between aqueous and vitreous, both watery, so despite its curvature it works across a much smaller step. *(The index-difference explanation is not taken from the course material; the deck gives the formula n = c/v on L7,8 p16 and prints no refractive-index value anywhere.)*\n\nThe unit itself is defined two slides later: the dioptre \"is the unit used for measuring lens power\", and the power of a lens in dioptres = **1 / focal length in metres** (L7,8 p24). That relation is what makes the 60 D figure meaningful — an eye of 60 D focuses parallel rays about 1/60 of a metre, roughly 17 mm, behind its principal plane, which is about where the retina of a 22–24.5 mm globe sits.\n\nThe practical use of these numbers is elsewhere in this chapter: Q18 asks what happens when the lens's 18–20 D is removed and not replaced, and the answer — high hypermetropia — is this slide read backwards.\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE MISPRINT REPAIRED.** The bank prints option A as \"Cornea is the major **contibutor**\"; the option above gives \"contributor\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The eye totals about 60 D: the cornea supplies 42–44 D and the lens 18–20 D, roughly two thirds and one third; a dioptre is the reciprocal of the focal length in metres.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.21 (Ch.2 Q25)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding light refraction in the eye except?",
    options:["The 2nd medium (cornea and lens) is optically denser","Light speed is increased as it passes into the 2nd medium","Light rays are focused on the macula","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Light slows down when it enters a denser medium — it does not speed up — and that slowing is precisely why it bends. B is the false statement.**\n\nThe deck gives one formula in 88 slides and it settles this: **n = c / v**, where n is the index of refraction, c the velocity of light in vacuum and v the velocity of light in the medium (L7,8 p16). Read the equation: for n to be greater than 1, v must be *less* than c. An optically denser medium is by definition one with a higher n, and a higher n means a lower velocity. Density and speed move in opposite directions, always.\n\n**A** is therefore true and is the premise B contradicts. The cornea and lens are optically denser than the air the light arrives from, which is what makes them refract at all; the deck illustrates the principle with its air/water figure, where the ray bends toward the normal on entering the denser medium (L7,8 p13, p17).\n\n**C** is true as the deck teaches it. Its camera analogy names the retina as the film (L7,8 p22), and its emmetropia schematic shows parallel rays converging exactly on the retina (L7,8 p18, p19). The macula is the retinal point where they must land for clear vision, and the astigmatism figures define all five subtypes by where the focal lines sit relative to the **fovea** (L7,8 p67) — so the deck itself uses the macular landmark as the reference plane. Strictly, an eye focuses whatever it is looking at onto the fovea and the rest of the field onto the rest of the retina; \"focused on the macula\" is the statement about the object of regard, and that is how the option is meant.\n\n**D, all of the above**, cannot stand once B is false.\n\nOne clarification the option wording invites: \"optically denser\" is not the same as \"physically denser\". Optical density is refractive index, not mass per volume, and the two do not always track each other. The eye's media happen to be both, so the distinction does not change the answer here. *(No refractive-index value appears anywhere in the deck — its NOT-COVERED register records \"not for cornea, aqueous, lens, vitreous, air or water\" — so the numbers behind this reasoning are not from the course material.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"n = c/v: a higher refractive index means light travels slower, so light slows on entering the optically denser cornea and lens, and that is what bends it onto the macula.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.21 (Ch.2 Q26)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Keratoconus (conical protrusion and thinning of the cornea) is a known cause of which of the following refractive errors?",
    options:["Myopia","Hyperopia","Astigmatism","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Keratoconus makes the cornea conical, and a conical surface has a different radius of curvature in every meridian — that is irregular astigmatism.** The lecture puts it there by name: on the astigmatism causes tree, the irregular branch reads \"**Corneal scars and Keratoconus**\" (L7,8 p64).\n\nThe mechanism is in the stem. Regular astigmatism has two principal meridians at right angles, each with its own uniform power, which is why a cylinder can correct it. In keratoconus the cornea thins and bulges into a cone, so curvature varies continuously and irregularly across the surface; there are no two orthogonal meridians to prescribe for. This is why the irregular arm of the treatment tree looks nothing like the regular arm: glasses only in mild degrees, **rigid gas permeable contact lenses** as the mainstay, and surgically **intracorneal ring segments** or **keratoplasty** (L7,8 p71) — the subject of Q17 of this chapter.\n\n⚠️ **A GENUINE DIVERGENCE INSIDE THE COURSE MATERIAL, RECORDED NOT RESOLVED.** The same lecture also names keratoconus as a cause of **myopia**. On the myopia causes slide, under curvature myopia: \"Increased curvature of the cornea (e.g., **keratoconus**) the lens (e.g., lenticonus)\" — the conjunction \"or\" is genuinely missing from the slide (L7,8 p42). So option **A** is not a fabricated distractor; it is the professor's own other teaching, and both statements are true of the real disease. A steepening cone increases the cornea's average power, which makes the eye myopic, *and* it destroys the regularity of that power, which makes the eye astigmatic. Clinically the keratoconic patient is usually both — progressive myopia with rapidly changing irregular astigmatism is the classic history.\n\nThe bank keys **C**, and the key is not disputed. The stem's own parenthesis is what tips it: it defines keratoconus as \"conical protrusion **and thinning**\" of the cornea, describing the shape rather than the power, and a conical shape is the astigmatic feature. Astigmatism is also the feature that dominates management, since it is the irregularity, not the myopia, that spectacles cannot fix.\n\n**B, hyperopia**, is the wrong direction on both counts — hyperopia of corneal origin comes from corneal **flattening**, a decreased curvature (L7,8 p53), which is the opposite of a cone. **D, none of the above**, is excluded by the p64 tree.\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE MISPRINT REPAIRED.** The bank prints \"which of the following **refreative** errors?\"; the stem above gives \"refractive\". The misspelling was verified at 2× zoom on a crop of the printed line, not read at page scale.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Keratoconus produces irregular astigmatism, correctable only by RGP lenses or corneal surgery; the same lecture also lists it under curvature myopia, and clinically it causes both.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.21 (Ch.2 Q27)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common form of astigmatism?",
    options:["Regular lenticular astigmatism","Irregular lenticular astigmatism","Regular corneal astigmatism","Irregular corneal astigmatism"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The lecture's causes tree answers this in two steps, and each step is labelled with its own frequency (L7,8 p64):**\n\n| Step | What the slide prints |\n|---|---|\n| Site | Lenticular astigmatism **(very rare)** vs Corneal astigmatism **(most common)** |\n| Regularity, within corneal | **Regular (more common)** vs Irregular (less common) |\n\nTake the commoner branch at each step — corneal, then regular — and the answer is **regular corneal astigmatism**. The definition slide says the same thing in prose: astigmatism is due to a non-spherical surface of \"the **cornea (more common)** or the lens\" (L7,8 p61).\n\nWhy the cornea rather than the lens: the cornea is the eye's principal refracting surface at 42–44 of its roughly 60 dioptres (L7,8 p23), so any departure from sphericity there has a large optical consequence, whereas the lens contributes less power and sits in a smaller index step. Ordinary corneal astigmatism is a normal anatomical variation — most corneas are slightly toric — which is why it is so common and why most of it is mild and asymptomatic (L7,8 p68).\n\n**A, regular lenticular astigmatism**, and **B, irregular lenticular astigmatism**, both fail at the first step: lenticular astigmatism is \"very rare\" and is a terminal box on the tree, not even subdivided into regular and irregular. (The option therefore describes a category the deck does not draw.) Lenticular astigmatism arises from lens tilt, subluxation or lenticonus, all uncommon.\n\n**D, irregular corneal astigmatism**, takes the right site and the wrong regularity. Irregular astigmatism is explicitly \"less common\" and has named pathological causes — corneal scars and keratoconus (L7,8 p64) — rather than being a variation of normal. Disease is rarer than anatomy.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Corneal astigmatism is much commoner than lenticular, and within it regular is commoner than irregular — so regular corneal astigmatism is the commonest form.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.21 (Ch.2 Q28)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-29', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract-astig',
    stem:"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of presbyopia?",
    options:["The far point of the eye becomes nearer","It is caused by hardening of the lens with age","It is usually unilateral","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hardening of the lens with age is the first of the lecture's two printed causes of presbyopia (L7,8 p74): \"Hardening (reduced deformability) of the lens with age\" and \"Reduced accommodative amplitude with age\".**\n\n**A** confuses the two cardinal points, and the confusion is worth unpicking because it is the single most useful distinction in this chapter. Presbyopia is defined as \"**Recession of the NEAR point** (i.e., it becomes farther from the eye) with age\" (L7,8 p73). It is the *near* point that moves, and it moves **away** from the eye, not nearer. The **far** point does not move at all: it is fixed by the eye's static refraction with accommodation at rest, and presbyopia is a failure of accommodation, not a change in static refraction. That is why the presbyope's distance vision is unaffected — the deck's own presbyopia table lists \"Distance vision remains unaffected\" for emmetropes (L7,8 p75). An emmetropic presbyope still has his far point at infinity (L7,8 p18).\n\n**C** is wrong because presbyopia is a property of the ageing crystalline lens, and both lenses age together. It is bilateral and symmetrical, and its onset is set by age rather than by anything local. A genuinely unilateral near-vision problem in a patient of presbyopic age is pointing at something else — an early unilateral cataract, an untreated anisometropia, or a third nerve or accommodative palsy — and should not be called presbyopia. *(The bilaterality point is not stated in the course material; the deck presents presbyopia throughout as a whole-person age process, \"a normal aging process (usually in persons 40 years of age or older)\" (L7,8 p73), and never discusses laterality.)*\n\n**D, all of the above**, fails on A and C.\n\nThe deck's clinical picture is worth having alongside the definition, because it is what the patient actually reports (L7,8 p75): emmetropes hold books farther away, have difficulty knitting and sewing, keep clear distance vision, and need more light for near tasks; low myopes (up to 4–5 D) take their glasses off to read; high myopes with their glasses on behave as emmetropes; and hyperopes become presbyopic earlier than 40 through accommodative insufficiency. Treatment is a spherical plus addition to the distance correction — reading glasses, bifocals or progressive lenses (L7,8 p81).\n\nThis question and Q21 of this chapter test the same slide from opposite directions: Q21 offers the *inverted* form of this cause (\"increased deformability\") as a distractor, and this one offers it correctly as the key.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Presbyopia is bilateral lens hardening with age that recedes the NEAR point; the far point does not move, which is why distance vision stays unaffected.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.21 (Ch.2 Q29)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-30', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Accommodative esotropia occurs on top of which of the following refractive errors?",
    options:["Myopia","Hypermetropia","Astigmatism","Presbyopia"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The lecture names accommodative esotropia twice, and both times it is under hyperopia** — in the clinical presentation of the young hyperope, who \"uncommonly develop[s] accommodative esotropia in childhood period\" (L7,8 p55), and again in the complications list, where squint is subdivided into \"apparent divergent squint\" and \"**accommodative esotropia**\" (L7,8 p58).\n\nThe mechanism is a chain of three links, and only hyperopia starts it. The hyperopic eye focuses parallel rays behind the retina (L7,8 p49), so the child must accommodate to see clearly — \"Accommodation by the ciliary muscle can correct hypermetropia in younger patients\" (L7,8 p54). Accommodation is neurologically yoked to convergence, so sustained accommodative effort drags the eyes inward. In a child with a large hyperopic error, the convergence that comes free with the accommodation he needs exceeds what fusion can hold, and one eye turns in — an **eso**tropia caused by accommodating, hence accommodative. *(The accommodation-convergence link is not taken from the course material; the deck states that accommodation compensates hyperopia and that accommodative esotropia complicates it, without naming the link between them.)*\n\n**A, myopia**, cannot produce it, because the myope's problem is too much power, not too little. He has no reason to accommodate for distance — accommodating would make his distance blur worse — so the convergence drive is never generated. Myopia is if anything associated with the opposite deviation.\n\n**C, astigmatism**, is not on the deck's squint list. Its complications are the symptom set on L7,8 p68 — blurred vision, narrowing of the palpebral fissure, asthenopia and headaches — and a cylinder does not create a sustained accommodative demand of the kind that yokes convergence.\n\n**D, presbyopia**, is excluded by age and by mechanism at once. Presbyopia begins at about 40 (L7,8 p73), long after the period in which accommodative esotropia develops, and it is a *loss* of accommodative power — the presbyope cannot generate the excess accommodation the mechanism requires.\n\nNote how this fits the rest of the chapter: Q7 asks whether the same complication is still possible in a 34-year-old and the answer is yes (accommodation is still being exerted), while amblyopia is not. The two questions test the difference between a complication limited by the developmental window and one limited only by whether the patient still accommodates.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Accommodative esotropia complicates hyperopia: the child accommodates to overcome the error and the yoked convergence turns an eye in — a mechanism myopia and presbyopia cannot produce.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.22 (Ch.2 Q30)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-31', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding treatment of hyperopia?",
    options:["It's indicated in all cases","It's indicated only in symptomatic or complicated cases","It's done through refractive surgery in young age","It maybe done using minus lenses","All of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hyperopia is treated when it causes trouble, not because it exists — and the lecture's clinical picture says so implicitly by describing the young hyperope who needs nothing.**\n\n\"Young age (1st, 2nd and 3rd decades of life): **usually do not require glasses for correction of far vision** (as hyperopia is compensated by accommodation)\" (L7,8 p55). A young hyperope with clear vision and no asthenopia is simply left alone; his own accommodation is doing the work a lens would do. Correction becomes indicated when the compensation starts costing him something — asthenopia with prolonged near work, blurred near vision in the fourth and fifth decades, or blurring of both distance and near above 50 (L7,8 p55, p56) — or when a complication appears: accommodative esotropia, apparent divergent squint, or the shallow-chamber predisposition to narrow-angle glaucoma (L7,8 p58). That is **B**, symptomatic or complicated.\n\n⚠️ The deck does not print the sentence \"treatment is indicated only in symptomatic or complicated cases\" in those words. It is the reading its own three-band clinical picture forces, and no other option survives. *(The explicit indication-threshold is not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**A, indicated in all cases**, contradicts the young-age band directly. It would mean prescribing glasses to an asymptomatic child who sees 6/6 — which the deck says is usually unnecessary. (There is a real exception it does not raise: a child with a *large* hyperopic error or an accommodative esotropia is fully corrected regardless of symptoms, precisely to prevent the squint and the amblyopia. That exception is a complication case, so it still sits under B.)\n\n**C, refractive surgery in young age**, misstates the timing. The deck's hyperopia treatment tree offers surgery — LASIK, and clear lens extraction — but as an alternative to glasses and contact lenses, not as the young-age option (L7,8 p59). Young hyperopes are the group least likely to need any correction at all, and operating on an error the patient's own accommodation is neutralising, in an eye whose refraction may still be changing, is the wrong order of treatment.\n\n**D, minus lenses**, is the direct optical error. Hyperopia needs converging power: \"Glasses — **Convex or plus lenses**\" (L7,8 p59), against \"Concave (Minus Lenses) → Myopia\" (L7,8 p32). A minus lens diverges (L7,8 p33) and would drive the focus further behind the retina. This is the same fact as Q6 of this chapter, where spherical concave glasses are the keyed exception.\n\n**E, all of the above**, fails on A, C and D.\n\n⚠️ **SOURCE NOTE.** The bank prints option D as \"It **maybe** done using minus lenses\" — one word where \"may be\" is meant. The option is reproduced above as printed, since the elision does not change its meaning and the option is false either way.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Hyperopia is corrected only when symptomatic or complicated — young hyperopes compensate by accommodating — and always with plus lenses, contact lenses or surgery, never minus lenses.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.22 (Ch.2 Q31)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c2-32', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-refract',
    stem:"Which structure in the eye provides the greatest refractive power?",
    options:["Lens","Cornea","Vitreous","Aqueous humor"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The cornea, at 42–44 of the eye's roughly 60 dioptres (L7,8 p23).** The lecture's optics tree prints the whole answer: cornea 42–44 D, lens 18–20 D, total approximately 60 D.\n\n**A, the lens**, is the intuitive answer and the reason the question is asked. The lens *looks* like the optical component — it is lens-shaped, it is adjustable, it is what a camera analogy points at (L7,8 p22) — but it supplies only about a third of the eye's power. What it uniquely provides is not magnitude but **variability**: it is the only element that can change power, which is accommodation (L7,8 p54), and losing it costs the eye its 18–20 D and leaves high hypermetropia (Q18 of this chapter).\n\nThe reason the cornea wins is the interface, not the shape. Refraction occurs where the refractive index changes, and its strength scales with the size of that change. The corneal front surface separates **air** from tissue, the largest index step in the eye; the lens is surrounded by aqueous in front and vitreous behind, both watery, so a much smaller step across a comparably curved surface yields much less power. *(The index-step explanation is not taken from the course material: the deck gives n = c/v on L7,8 p16 and prints no index value for any ocular medium.)*\n\n**C, vitreous**, and **D, aqueous humor**, are transparent media rather than refracting surfaces in any meaningful sense. They transmit light and maintain the eye's shape and pressure; because their indices are close to each other and to the lens's surroundings, the interfaces they form contribute negligibly. The deck's own tree names only two optical elements — cornea and lens — and neither humour appears on it. (The vitreous \"comprises about 1/3 of the eye volume\", per this bank's own chapter 1, `opmcq-c1-12` — volume, not power.)\n\nThe clinical corollary is why corneal refractive surgery works at all: reshaping the cornea by a few micrometres changes the eye's dominant refracting surface, which is why LASIK and PRK can correct several dioptres, and why corneal disease — keratoconus, scarring, oedema — has such a large refractive consequence (L7,8 p47, p59, p64).\n\nThis is Q25 of this chapter reduced to a single fact, and it is the last question of the chapter.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The cornea supplies 42–44 of the eye's ~60 dioptres because its front surface separates air from tissue; the lens contributes 18–20 D but is the only variable element.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.22 (Ch.2 Q32)' }
